-- check/bad: §10.1's other arm — a `where` name that is NOT well known,
-- on a receiver whose type nothing ever determines (§7.2, A.66).
--
-- `Box 1 2 == Box 3 4` resolves `==` to this module's own `eq`, whose
-- `where` clause asks the payload for `describe`. The payload is an
-- integer literal nobody ever uses at `Int` or at `Float`, so its type
-- stays `number`, and `Solve.settleUndetermined` has to answer the slot
-- with something. For `eq` and `compare` it can — A.66's bridge, which is
-- what makes `[] == []` compile — because those mean the same thing at
-- every type and no value of an undetermined one ever reaches the function
-- handed over. For `describe` it cannot: there is no type to look the name
-- up in, and inventing a function would be inventing a meaning.
--
-- It used to answer NOTHING, in silence. The check exited 0, the evidence
-- slot stayed empty, and the emitted call to `eq` was one argument short —
-- caught only by `Lower.evidenceShapeOk`, which reports an `internal`
-- compiler bug about a program whose only fault is that it never says
-- which type it means. Adding `Box 1 2 == Box (Id 3) (Id 4)`, or
-- annotating the literals, is all it takes to make this program legal, and
-- neither is something an `internal` tells an author to do.
pub type Box a
    = Box a a


pub eq : Box a, Box a -> Bool
    where a.describe : a, Int -> String
eq p q =
    case p of
        Box x _ ->
            case q of
                Box y _ ->
                    x.describe 1 == y.describe 2


pub same : Bool
same =
    Box 1 2 == Box 3 4
